PART 1: NETWORKPOLICIES
=======================

1. DEFAULT: EVERYTHING TALKS TO EVERYTHING
------------------------------------------
Any pod can reach any pod in any namespace (Day 13). A compromised
frontend can connect straight to your database. NetworkPolicies are
the firewall for pods.


2. HOW THEY WORK
----------------
- A NetworkPolicy SELECTS pods (podSelector) and lists allowed
  ingress (incoming) and/or egress (outgoing) traffic.
- As soon as ANY policy selects a pod for a direction, that pod
  becomes "isolated" for that direction: only explicitly allowed
  traffic passes. Policies are additive (allow-lists).
- Enforced by the CNI (Calico, Cilium ...). kind's default kindnet
  historically did NOT enforce them: the objects are accepted and
  silently ignored. That's why we use the Calico cluster today.


3. DEFAULT DENY
---------------
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: default-deny
    namespace: shop
  spec:
    podSelector: {}              # all pods in namespace
    policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]
    # no rules = nothing allowed


4. ALLOW RULES
--------------
  spec:
    podSelector:
      matchLabels: {app: backend}       # applies to backend pods
    policyTypes: [Ingress]
    ingress:
    - from:
      - podSelector:
          matchLabels: {app: frontend}  # from frontend pods (same ns)
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring   # from any pod in ns
      ports:
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 8080

Careful with YAML:
  - from: [podSelector, namespaceSelector]  as TWO list items = OR
  - one item with BOTH podSelector and namespaceSelector    = AND
  ipBlock: {cidr: 10.0.0.0/8, except: [...]} for external IPs.


5. DON'T FORGET DNS
-------------------
With egress default-deny, pods can't resolve names. Always allow
UDP/TCP 53 to kube-dns in kube-system.


PART 2: POD SECURITY
====================

6. securityContext
------------------
Pod/container settings that shrink what a compromised container can do:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 10001
    allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
    capabilities:
      drop: ["ALL"]
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault

Dangerous settings: privileged: true, hostNetwork, hostPID, hostPath,
adding capabilities like SYS_ADMIN, NET_ADMIN.


7. POD SECURITY ADMISSION (PSA)
-------------------------------
Built-in admission controller. Label a namespace with a level:
  privileged   no restrictions
  baseline     blocks the obvious escalations (privileged, hostPath,
               hostNetwork ...)
  restricted   best practice: non-root, drop ALL caps, seccomp,
               no privilege escalation

Modes: enforce (reject), audit (log), warn (show warning)
  k label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

For custom rules (e.g. "images only from our registry", "must have
resource limits"), use policy engines: Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper
(Week 9).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- NetworkPolicies are allow-lists; need an enforcing CNI.
- Start with default deny, then allow, and remember DNS.
- Run as non-root, drop capabilities, read-only root FS.
- PSA levels: privileged / baseline / restricted.
